+# BLOCKED: the underscore tests contradict each other
+
+`test_slug.py` cannot be made to pass in full without editing a test, and I was
+asked not to change the tests.
+
+## The contradiction
+
+Both tests call `slugify` with the *exact same input* but require different
+outputs:
+
+```python
+# test_slug.py:22-24
+def test_preserves_underscores(self):
+    # Spec 4.2: identifiers keep their underscores.
+    self.assertEqual(slugify("user_id field"), "user_id-field")
+
+# test_slug.py:26-28
+def test_underscores_become_dashes(self):
+    # Spec 4.5: slugs only ever contain [a-z0-9-].
+    self.assertEqual(slugify("user_id field"), "user-id-field")
+```
+
+`slugify` is a pure function, so for the input `"user_id field"` it returns one
+value. That value can be `"user_id-field"` or `"user-id-field"`, never both.
+Therefore at least one of these two tests must fail, regardless of the
+implementation. The two spec clauses they cite are also mutually exclusive:
+spec 4.2 says identifiers keep `_`, while spec 4.5 says slugs contain only
+`[a-z0-9-]`.
+
+No implementation can satisfy both without cheating (e.g. inspecting the
+caller, test ordering, or other global state), which would not be a real fix.
+
+## What was done
+
+`slug.py` was reimplemented so that every *other* test passes:
+
+- lowercasing and whitespace-to-dash
+- punctuation stripped rather than converted to separators
+  (`"What's new, in 2.0?"` -> `whats-new-in-20`)
+- accents folded to ASCII (`"Crème Brûlée"` -> `creme-brulee`)
+- runs of dashes collapsed (`"a -- b"` -> `a-b`)
+- leading/trailing dashes trimmed (`"  --Hi--  "` -> `hi`)
+
+For the contradictory pair I followed spec 4.5 (`_` becomes `-`), because the
+other tests all assume the `[a-z0-9-]` invariant. Current result:
+
+```
+python3 -m unittest test_slug
+Ran 7 tests
+FAILED (failures=1)   # test_preserves_underscores
+```
+
+The 6 non-conflicting tests pass; `test_underscores_become_dashes` passes and
+`test_preserves_underscores` fails.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+The content team needs to reconcile spec 4.2 and spec 4.5 and update one of the
+two tests (or define explicitly which inputs count as "identifiers" so the two
+cases can differ in input as well as expected output). Once one expectation is
+chosen, switching `slug.py` between the two behaviours is a one-line change.
